INT. GOLD STANDARD PAWN - DAY

A narrow shop crammed floor to ceiling: guitars on hooks, a glass case of watches, power tools in rows, a stuffed marlin over the register. A buzzer on the door. Dusty light.

IGNATIUS KOWAL, 60s, owner, bald and bespectacled, sits behind the counter with a jeweler's loupe. DETECTIVE NASRIN FARHADI, 40s, in a wrinkled blazer, leans on the glass case with a notebook. At a rack of coats, MAUREEN PELL, 50s, a regular, sorts through winter jackets she has no intention of buying.

The buzzer sounds. A YOUNG MAN, 20s, in a hooded sweatshirt, hurries in with a pillowcase. He sees the detective and stops.

YOUNG MAN
I'll come back.

IGNATIUS
You'll stay. Everybody stays at Gold Standard.

The young man hesitates. Ignatius lowers the loupe. The detective does not move, but her eyes follow him.

IGNATIUS (CONT'D)
That's Trey Mackenzie. His mother is Maureen's cousin.

MAUREEN
Second cousin.

TREY
(to the floor)
I just want to pawn this.

DETECTIVE FARHADI
What is it?

TREY
A... lamp.

IGNATIUS
Put it on the counter, Trey.

Trey sets the pillowcase down. Ignatius opens it, peers in, and sighs. A radio on the shelf crackles.

DISPATCH (O.S.)
(over radio)
All units, burglary on Delmar Street, possible suspect on foot, gray hoodie.

Nobody speaks. Trey's hand drifts toward the pillowcase. Detective Farhadi closes her notebook.

DETECTIVE FARHADI
Trey, how about we take a walk?
